+// Coding Harness's `debug` tool drives a real debugger (DAP). A bugfix's
+// diagnosis gains observed program state instead of reasoning from source alone;
+// the conductor (skill) and the developer (agent body) both carry it.
+export const DEBUGGER_GUIDANCE = `### Diagnose a defect with the debugger
+
+In a bugfix the cause comes from the running program, not from reading the
+source, however obvious the source makes it look. Once the failure reproduces
+(the failing test or command) and the \`debug\` tool has an adapter for the
+language (debugpy for Python, installed in the project's \`.venv\`; the
+JavaScript debug adapter for Node; lldb or gdb for native code; dlv for Go), you
+MUST, before any edit to source:
+
+1. launch the reproduction under the \`debug\` tool;
+2. set a breakpoint on the line where the fault is suspected and run to it;
+3. read the variables there.
+
+Write the observed values (for example \`retries = 0\` where the loop expects 3)
+into the diagnosis the stage records, the requirements for the defect or the
+code-generation plan, so the approver sees the evidence. Skip the debugger only
+when no adapter applies, and say so in the diagnosis.
